BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Kelley School of Business
DTSTART:20261008T160000Z
DTEND:20261008T173000Z
SUMMARY:Frontlines & Futures: The United Nations General Assembly Debrief
 —Power\, Rhetoric\, and the Reality of Global Governance
DESCRIPTION:Every September\, world leaders\, presidents\, and prime mini
 sters descend on New York City for the United Nations General Assembly. I
 t is the closest thing the world has to a global parliament\, where all 1
 93 member countries get a stage to announce their priorities\, air grieva
 nces\, and strike backroom deals.  But once the grand speeches wrap up an
 d the motorcades leave\, what actually happens to those big promises? How
  does high-level political rhetoric translate into real\, actionable poli
 cy?  If you attended the America&#8217\;s Role in the World conference on
  campus and wanted to pull back the curtain on how international strategi
 es are negotiated\, this session is for you.  Launched by the Hamilton Lu
 gar School to bring emerging policy thinkers together for candid\, real-w
 orld conversations\, Frontlines &amp\; Futures is a fast-paced\, interact
 ive salon built entirely around small-group dialogue and real-world analy
 sis\, not a passive lecture. Together\, we will pull actual policy statem
 ents and video prompts straight from the United Nations floor to test how
  these international institutions function\, where they fracture\, and ho
 w countries negotiate when compliance is entirely voluntary.No time for a
  full 90-minute session? No problem. This is a dynamic\, open-door salon\
 , you are completely welcome to drop in\, grab food\, participate for a b
 it\, and head out whenever your schedule requires.  &#160\;Who Is This Fo
 r?Graduate Students: (Primary focus) Move past seminar theories to pressu
 re-test actual diplomatic realities.    Undergrads\, Faculty\, Staff\, &a
 mp\; Alumni: Anyone ready to see how technical realities collide with glo
 bal politics.    What We're Stress-Testing:The Tech Deficit: Global respo
 nses to borderless cyber warfare\, digital sovereignty\, and unregulated 
 AI.    Shifting Alliances: How rising economic blocs leverage the UN to c
 hallenge Western influence.    Actionable Tradecraft: Practical steps the
  U.S. and its allies can take when there is no global police force.      
 This interactive salon gives you a structured\, informal space to sharpen
  the exact risk-assessment and policy reasoning skills used by profession
 als in the field. Turnout is kept small to guarantee everyone has a voice
 .
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-path-to-node="4">  Every September\,
  world leaders\, presidents\, and prime ministers descend on New York Cit
 y for the <strong data-path-to-node="4" data-index-in-node="97">United Na
 tions General Assembly</strong>. It is the closest thing the world has to
  a global parliament\, where all 193 member countries get a stage to anno
 unce their priorities\, air grievances\, and strike backroom deals.</p><p
  data-path-to-node="5">  But once the grand speeches wrap up and the moto
 rcades leave\, what actually happens to those big promises? How does high
 -level political rhetoric translate into real\, actionable policy?</p><p 
 data-path-to-node="6">  If you attended the <i data-path-to-node="6" data
 -index-in-node="20">America&#8217\;s Role in the World</i> conference on 
 campus and wanted to pull back the curtain on how international strategie
 s are negotiated\, this session is for you.</p><p data-path-to-node="7"> 
  Launched by the Hamilton Lugar School to bring emerging policy thinkers 
 together for candid\, real-world conversations\, <strong data-path-to-nod
 e="7" data-index-in-node="119">Frontlines &amp\; Futures</strong> is a fa
 st-paced\, interactive salon built entirely around small-group dialogue a
 nd real-world analysis\, not a passive lecture. Together\, we will pull a
 ctual policy statements and video prompts straight from the United Nation
 s floor to test how these international institutions function\, where the
 y fracture\, and how countries negotiate when compliance is entirely volu
 ntary.</p><p data-path-to-node="8"><strong data-path-to-node="8" data-ind
 ex-in-node="0">No time for a full 90-minute session? No problem. This is 
 a dynamic\, open-door salon\, you are completely welcome to drop in\, gra
 b food\, participate for a bit\, and head out whenever your schedule requ
 ires.</strong></p><p data-path-to-node="8">  &#160\;</p><h3 data-path-to-
 node="2"><strong data-path-to-node="2" data-index-in-node="0">Who Is This
  For?</strong></h3><ul data-path-to-node="3"><li><p data-path-to-node="3\
 ,0\,0"><strong data-path-to-node="3\,0\,0" data-index-in-node="0">Graduat
 e Students:</strong> (Primary focus) Move past seminar theories to pressu
 re-test actual diplomatic realities.    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node
 ="3\,1\,0"><strong data-path-to-node="3\,1\,0" data-index-in-node="0">Und
